\begin{keyword}
lattice models \sep von Mises--Fisher distribution \sep orientational order \sep entropy \sep unsupervised anomaly detection \sep spatial smoothing
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}
Many pattern recognition problems put a vector on every site of a grid: orientation fields of ordered media, and embeddings of the patches of an image. Averaged order parameters say how ordered such a lattice is, not where its organization is unusual. Finding unusual local organization without labels, and saying how unusual it is in the language of order and entropy, is the problem addressed here. Unsupervised anomaly detection has been developed in other fields: in astronomy for large surveys \citep{Baron2017,Lochner2021}, and in medical imaging mostly as detection of deviation from a healthy appearance with generative models \citep{Schlegl2019}.

MyUncle \citep{MyUncle2026} is a simulation framework for a maintenance lattice: rotors on a square lattice whose order is eroded by a state-dependent flux and restored by repair, with an order-entropy bridge that links the length of the neighbors' mean unit vector to a von Mises entropy. This paper makes four contributions. (1) It extends the local order and entropy quantities from the plane to unit vectors in $d$ dimensions (the von Mises--Fisher distribution) and verifies that they equal MyUncle's at $d=2$ against the unmodified code. (2) It builds a label-free score that judges the rarity of the state of a site at several prevalence scales and smooths it over the lattice. (3) It tests the score, with frozen settings and four baselines, on lattices simulated with MyUncle (damaged sites as ground truth) and on synthetic stained-tissue images, where the sites are image patches described by an embedding; the results are mixed and are reported as such. (4) It provides an open pipeline \citep{MyUncleVision2026}. All evidence is simulated; no experimental image was analyzed.

\section{Order and entropy of a lattice of unit vectors}
Let $u_j$ be the unit vector at site $j$ of a lattice, $N_i$ the valid neighbors of site $i$ (four, on a square lattice), and $d$ the dimension of the vectors. MyUncle's local disorder is one minus the length of the mean of the neighbors' unit vectors; the same definition holds in any dimension:
\begin{equation}
R_i=\Bigl|\tfrac{1}{|N_i|}\textstyle\sum_{j\in N_i}u_j\Bigr|,\qquad D_i=1-R_i .
\end{equation}
The concentration $\kappa_i$ of the von Mises--Fisher distribution on $S^{d-1}$ \citep{Banerjee2005,Mardia2000} with mean resultant length $R_i$ solves
\begin{equation}
A_d(\kappa)=\frac{I_{d/2}(\kappa)}{I_{d/2-1}(\kappa)}=R_i ,
\end{equation}
and the entropy of that distribution and the information held in the local order are
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
S_{\mathrm{vMF}}(\kappa,d)&=-\bigl(\tfrac d2-1\bigr)\ln\kappa+\tfrac d2\ln(2\pi)\\&\quad+\ln I_{d/2-1}(\kappa)-\kappa A_d(\kappa),
\end{split}\qquad H_\chi=S_{\mathrm{unif}}-S_{\mathrm{vMF}},
\end{equation}
with $S_{\mathrm{unif}}$ the entropy of the uniform distribution on the sphere. For $d=2$ these are MyUncle's $U=A(\kappa)=I_1/I_0$, $S(\kappa)$ and $H_\chi$. The tests compare them with the vendored, unmodified core (Fig.~\ref{fig:bridge}): the local disorder on a periodic lattice agrees to $10^{-12}$ and the entropy to $10^{-9}$. With a neighborhood of four sites the resultant length is a noisy estimate of the local order; this is a stated limitation.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig_bridge.png}
\caption{(a) The order-information bridge in $d$ dimensions, per dimension: MyUncle's $H_\chi$ ($d=2$, red) and the von Mises--Fisher generalization for $d=2$, 16, 64 and 384. (b) Difference between the $d=2$ entropy and the vendored MyUncle function.}
\label{fig:bridge}
\end{figure*}

\section{A label-free score on the lattice}
\textbf{Embeddings.} For images, a patch is a $224\times224$ px window (0.5~$\mu$m/px) of the image; it is encoded either by DINOv2 ViT-S/14 \citep{Oquab2023} (384-d, pretrained on natural images, no fine-tuning) or by a deterministic handcrafted encoder of color, gradient-orientation and texture statistics (64-d) built on color deconvolution \citep{Ruifrok2001}. Embeddings are centered, reduced by a cohort PCA (16 components, scikit-learn; \citealp{Pedregosa2011}) and normalized to unit length. For simulated lattices the vector of a site is the rotor $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$, optionally with the local disorder $D$ as a third coordinate; the intact flag is never part of it.

\textbf{Score.} Appearance rarity $p_{\mathrm{emb}}$ is the cohort percentile of the mean cosine distance to the $k$ nearest sites of a reference sample of the cohort (same-image neighbors within six lattice steps excluded). Because the prevalence of an interesting state is not known in advance, it is evaluated with $k$ equal to 0.5, 1, 2, 5 and 10\% of the reference sample and the highest rank counts. Two further rank-based axes, state-wise disorder $p_{\mathrm{het}}$ (the rank of $D_i$ among sites of the same $k$-means prototype state) and contact rarity $p_{\mathrm{adj}}$ (the rarity of the states a site touches), are diagnostics. The novelty of a site is the mean over its $3\times3$ lattice window $W_i$ of a weighted probabilistic OR of the axes,
\begin{equation}
N_i=\tfrac{1}{|W_i|}\textstyle\sum_{j\in W_i}q_j,\quad q_j=1-\prod_{a}(1-p_{a,j})^{w_a},
\end{equation}
with the frozen weights $(w_{\mathrm{emb}},w_{\mathrm{het}},w_{\mathrm{adj}})=(1,0,0)$: the default score is the smoothed multi-scale appearance rarity. The disorder and contact axes were part of the original design; they are kept as options because their effect depended on the embedding (Section~\ref{sec:res}). Candidates are the top fraction of $N_i$; connected candidates form regions, clustered across images into ranked findings with explanations made of measured numbers. \citet{MyUncleVision2026} gives the pipeline, including a bridge that simulates any MyUncle preset and analyzes its states.

\section{Evaluation design}
All settings of the score were frozen on development data (\texttt{results/frozen\_settings.json}, written before the held-out data existed), and the held-out data were evaluated once. The baselines are isolation forest, local outlier factor, nearest-neighbor distance ($k=10$) and Mahalanobis distance on the same embeddings, each raw and smoothed in the same way as the proposed score (percentile rank, then the $3\times3$ window); implementations are from scikit-learn. Metrics are AUROC and average precision (AP) against the ground truth; for the top 3\% of scores on images, the spatial coherence (the share in connected groups of at least three patches) and the region recall (planted instances containing a candidate).

\textbf{Simulated lattices.} MyUncle's kinetic Monte Carlo (unmodified core) was run from a damaged start; snapshots at steps 20, 40 and 60 of $48\times48$ sites were analyzed as images whose sites are patches. The damaged sites (sites that lost their coupling) are the ground truth. The development set is one preset (damaged share @DEVPREV@; four runs); the held-out set is three other presets (damaged share @HELDPREV@; four runs each). The presets are model parameter sets that MyUncle labels with application names (here polymer photodegradation, liquid crystal and magnetic storage decay); they are not fitted to measurements.

\textbf{Synthetic images.} No real image was available. Synthetic stained-tissue images ($4480\times4480$ px) with tubule-like rings, glomerulus-like discs, vessels, stain variation, planted anomalies and artifacts imitate what the method measures. A development set (8 images, @DEVIMGPREV@ of patches anomalous) and a held-out set (16 images, three anomaly types never used in development, @HELDIMGPREV@ anomalous, stain shifts) were written by the author of the method, which limits what they can show.

\section{Results}\label{sec:res}
\subsection{Simulated lattices}
\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Simulated MyUncle lattices, damaged sites as ground truth. Held-out presets: AUROC and AP with the rotor embedding (columns 2, 3) and the rotor + $D$ embedding (4, 5); AP on the development preset (6, 7).}
\label{tab:sim}
\centering\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Method & AUROC & AP & AUROC & AP & AP$_\mathrm{dev}$ & AP$_\mathrm{dev}$\\
\midrule
@TABSIM@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
Figure~\ref{fig:maps} shows one simulated run. With the rotor alone, the frozen score was no better than smoothed isolation forest or nearest-neighbor distance (held-out AP @S_AP@, AUROC @S_AUROC@) and clearly below the unsmoothed baselines (Mahalanobis AP @S_MAHA@, isolation forest @S_IF@; Table~\ref{tab:sim}). Smoothing lowered the AP of every baseline on both sets (isolation forest @S_IF@ to @S_IFSM@ on the held-out set): the damaged sites are scattered, not compact regions that a $3\times3$ window preserves. Adding the local disorder as a feature raised the AP of the frozen score on both sets (development @S_DEV_R@ to @S_DEV_RD@, held-out @S_AP@ to @S_HELD_RD@); on the held-out presets it was then within 0.02 of the best AP, tied with smoothed isolation forest, while on the development preset an unsmoothed isolation forest was clearly better (@S_DEV_IF@ against @S_DEV_RD@). The disorder map alone ranked the damaged sites poorly (held-out AP @S_DALONE@), so the gain comes from combining it with the state of the site. Intervals (95\%, resampling whole runs) are in the result files; they overlap for methods whose averages are close. The damaged share of three of the four parameter sets (about 10, 30 and 26\%; the fourth is 8\%) is near or above the largest prevalence scale of the rarity score (10\%), a limit of the frozen settings that this testbed exposes.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{fig_bridge_maps.png}
\caption{A MyUncle simulation analyzed with MyUncleVision (chiral scaffold preset, $32\times32$ sites). Rows: rotor angle, local disorder $D$, order information $H_\chi$ ($d=2$) and novelty of the frozen score; columns: steps 0, 15, 30, 45 and 60. Novelty is a percentile rank relative to the snapshots shown and has not been validated against known defect locations.}
\label{fig:maps}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Synthetic images}
An earlier default (three-axis OR at one rarity scale, no smoothing) had an AUROC of @OLD_HC@ with handcrafted features and @OLD_DINO@ with DINOv2, below chance: the planted states covered 3.1\% of the patches, more than the 1\% scale, and the DINOv2 embeddings of the planted regions are more homogeneous than normal ones, so the disorder and contact axes point the wrong way. Judging rarity at several scales fixed the appearance axis. On the development images, smoothing raised the spatial coherence of the frozen score from @COH0_HC@ to @COH1_HC@ (handcrafted) and from @COH0_D@ to @COH1_D@ (DINOv2), and smoothing raised the coherence of every baseline in all four cases; but a smoothed Mahalanobis baseline scored higher in AP than the frozen score (@DEV_MS_HC@ and @DEV_MS_D@ against @DEV_F_HC@ and @DEV_F_D@, handcrafted and DINOv2).

\begin{table*}[t]
\caption{Held-out synthetic images (settings frozen beforehand, evaluated once): AUROC, AP, spatial coherence (coh.) and region recall (rec.), handcrafted features (left) and DINOv2 (right).}
\label{tab:img}
\centering\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
Method & AUROC & AP & coh. & rec. & AUROC & AP & coh. & rec.\\
\midrule
@TABIMG@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

On the held-out images with DINOv2 the frozen score was the best of all methods in AP (@H_AP_D@, AUROC @H_AUROC_D@, coherence @H_COH_D@, region recall @H_REC_D@), ahead of smoothed Mahalanobis (@H_MS_D@) and of the unsmoothed multi-scale appearance (@H_U_D@; Table~\ref{tab:img}). With handcrafted features it did badly (AP @H_AP_HC@, region recall @H_REC_HC@) although its AUROC was @H_AUROC_HC@ and its coherence @H_COH_HC@: the anomalies are small (about five patches), and a $3\times3$ window mixes each with normal neighbors and dilutes the ranks. The choice to smooth, made on the development images, therefore did not generalize across embeddings. The lattice axes behaved as on the development images: with smoothing they raised the AP with handcrafted features (@H_T3_HC@ against @H_AP_HC@) and lowered it with DINOv2 (@H_T3_D@ against @H_AP_D@), so they are diagnostics, not part of the default score. A coarser patch size was tolerated with DINOv2 and not with handcrafted features, and changing the smoothing or the axes changed the flagged set almost completely: these are design choices, not noise.

