\label{fig:ex}
\end{figure*}

\section{Limitations and future work}
The lattices and the images are simulated and were written by the author of the method (the simulation is MyUncle's, with an easy kind of anomaly, and its parameter sets are not fitted to measurements); nothing here shows that the method finds anything in an experimental system. The held-out sets were used once but are of the same family as the development sets, four runs per parameter set are few, the two embeddings disagree on whether smoothing helps, and a single Gaussian model scored higher than the frozen score on the development data. Novelty is not a defect: a rare state may be a rare preparation, an instrument or a known feature that is merely uncommon. Planned: validation on experimental micrographs of ordered materials with independently established defects and on public histology, with settings, baselines and endpoints frozen and published before an independent set is analyzed once, blinded expert review, bootstrap intervals over images, a second synthetic generator, other spatial filters, and fitting the MyUncle parameter sets to measured data. If no advantage over the baselines appears, that result will be reported.

\section{Conclusions}
The local disorder and von Mises--Fisher order information of MyUncle generalize to lattices of unit vectors in $d$ dimensions and equal MyUncle's at $d=2$. A label-free score built on them was tested on simulated lattices and on synthetic images with frozen settings and four baselines. The results are mixed: smoothing helps when anomalies form regions and hurts when they do not, adding the local disorder as a feature helped on the simulated lattices, and a simple Mahalanobis baseline was a strong competitor. All evidence is simulated; experimental validation is the next step.

